// SR 281.1 — Title Four Debt Enforcement by Realising a Pledge
// generated by src/parse.py. Do not edit by hand.

:NOTE
English is not an official language of the Swiss Confederation. This translation is provided for information purposes only and has no legal force.

:PATH
A. Request for debt enforcement
|

:INFO
Art. 151
1 Any person who initiates debt enforcement proceedings for a claim secured by a pledge (Art. 37) must, in addition to the details listed in Article 67, specify the pledged asset in the request for debt enforcement. In addition, the following must be stated in the request where applicable:
a. the name of the third party who created the pledge or acquired ownership of the pledged asset;
[tab] b. the use of pledged immovable property as a family home (Art. 169 CC) or as a joint home (Art. 14 Same-Sex Partnership Act of 18 June 2004) for the debtor or the third party.
2 If a creditor initiates debt enforcement proceedings on the basis of a pledge on which a third party has a subordinate pledge (Art. 886 CC), the creditor must notify the third party of the initiation of the debt enforcement proceedings.
|

:NOTE
Art. 151 — Amended by No I of the FA of 16 Dec. 1994, in force since 1 Jan. 1997 (AS 1995 1227; BBl 1991 III 1).
Para. 1 let. tab — Amended by Annex No 16 of the Same-Sex Partnership Act of 18 June 2004, in force since 1 Jan. 2007 (AS 2005 5685; BBl 2003 1288).
Para. 1 let. tab — SR 210
Para. 1 let. tab — SR 211.231

:PATH
B. Summons for payment

:PATH
1. Content. Notice to tenants and lessees
|

:INFO
Art. 152
1 On receipt of the request for debt enforcement, the debt enforcement office shall issue a summons for payment in accordance with Article 69, but with the following special features:
The payment period to be set for the debtor is one month in the case of a lien on property, six months in the case of a mortgage.
The warning states that if the debtor neither complies with nor files an objection to the summons for payment, the pledge will be realised.
2 If immovable property is subject to a lease or usufructuary lease and the enforcing pledgee requests that liability under the pledge be extended to include the rent claims (Art. 806 CC), the debt enforcement office shall notify the tenants or lessees of the initiation of debt enforcement proceedings and instruct them to pay the rent that is due to the debt enforcement office.
|

:NOTE
Para. 1 — Amended by No I of the FA of 16 Dec. 1994, in force since 1 Jan. 1997 (AS 1995 1227; BBl 1991 III 1).
Para. 2 — SR 210
Para. 2 — Inserted by Art. 58 Final Title CC (AS 24 233 Art. 60 Final Title CC; BBl 1904 IV 1; 1907 VI 367). Amended by No I of the FA of 16 Dec. 1994, in force since 1 Jan. 1997 (AS 1995 1227; BBl 1991 III 1).

:PATH
2. Official copies. Status of a third-party owner of the pledged asset
|

:INFO
Art. 153
1 The summons for payment shall be issued in accordance with Article 70.
2 The debt enforcement office shall also serve a summons for payment on the following persons:
a. a third party who has created the pledge or acquired ownership of the pledged asset;
[tab] b. the spouse, the registered partner of the debtor or the third party if pledged immovable property serves as a family home (Art. 169 CC) or as a joint home (Art. 14 Same-Sex Partnership Act of 18 June 2004).
The third party and the spouse may file an objection to the summons in the same way as the debtor.
2bis The persons referred to in paragraph 2 may file an objection to the summons in the same way as the debtor.
3 If the third party has initiated redemption proceedings (Arts 828 and 829 CC), immovable property may only be realised if the creditor instituting the proceedings proves to the debt enforcement office that he is still the creditor to a mortgage on the property for the claim being enforced.
|

:INFO
Art. 153 (continued)
4 In all other respects, the provisions of Articles 71–86 apply with regard to the summons for payment and objection to the summons.
|

:NOTE
Para. 2 let. tab — Amended by Annex No 16 of the Same-Sex Partnership Act of 18 June 2004, in force since 1 Jan. 2007 (AS 2005 5685; BBl 2003 1288).
Para. 2 let. tab — SR 210
Para. 2 let. tab — SR 211.231
Para. 2 — Amended by No I of the FA of 16 Dec. 1994, in force since 1 Jan. 1997 (AS 1995 1227; BBl 1991 III 1).
Para. 2bis — Inserted by Annex No 16 of the Same-Sex Partnership Act of 18 June 2004, in force since 1 Jan. 2007 (AS 2005 5685; BBl 2003 1288).
Para. 3 — Inserted by Art. 58 Final Title CC (AS 24 233 Art. 60 Final Title CC; BBl 1904 IV 1; 1907 VI 367). Amended by No I of the FA of 16 Dec. 1994, in force since 1 Jan. 1997 (AS 1995 1227; BBl 1991 III 1).
Para. 4 — Originally para. 3.

:PATH
C. Objection to the summons. Cancellation of the notice to tenants and lessees
|

:INFO
Art. 153a
1 If an objection to the summons for payment is filed, the creditor may, within ten days of receiving notice of the objection, demand that the objection be set aside or file an action for recognition of the claim or for establishment of the pledge.
2 If the creditor is unsuccessful in having the objection set aside, he may file an action within ten days of receiving notice of the decision.
3 If he does not comply with these deadlines, notice to tenants and lessees shall be cancelled.
|

:NOTE
Art. 153a — Inserted by No I of the FA of 16 Dec. 1994, in force since 1 Jan. 1997 (AS 1995 1227; BBl 1991 III 1).
Para. 2 — Term pursuant to Annex 1 No II 17 of the Civil Procedure Code of 19 Dec. 2008, in force since 1 Jan. 2011 (AS 2010 1739; BBl 2006 7221). This amendment has been made throughout the text.

:PATH
D. Deadlines for realisation
|

:INFO
Art. 154
1 The creditor may request the realisation of a pledged chattel no earlier than one month and no later than one year, and the realisation of a mortgage no earlier than six months and no later than two years after service of the summons for payment. If an objection to the summons is filed, these time limits shall be suspended between the initiation and conclusion of any court proceedings brought as a result.
2 If the application for realisation is not filed or withdrawn and not renewed within the statutory period, the debt enforcement shall lapse.
|

:NOTE
Para. 1 — Amended by No I of the FA of 16 Dec. 1994, in force since 1 Jan. 1997 (AS 1995 1227; BBl 1991 III 1).

:PATH
E. Realisation procedure

:PATH
1. Initiation
|

:INFO
Art. 155
1 If the creditor has made the request for realisation, Articles 97 paragraph 1, 102 paragraph 3, 103 and 106–109 apply to the pledge mutatis mutandis.
2 The debt enforcement office shall notify the debtor of the request for realisation within three days.
|

:NOTE
Para. 1 — Amended by No I of the FA of 16 Dec. 1994, in force since 1 Jan. 1997 (AS 1995 1227; BBl 1991 III 1).

:PATH
2. Procedure
|

:INFO
Art. 156
1 Articles 122–143b apply to realisation. However, the auction terms (Art. 135) shall stipulate that the share of the sale price due to the pledgee be paid in cash unless the parties involved agree otherwise. They shall also stipulate that the encumbrance on the property that existed in favour of the debtor be deleted from the land register.
2 In the event of separate realisation, pledge instruments in the name of the owner or in bearer form and pledged by the landowner are reduced to the amount of the proceeds of realisation.
|

:NOTE
Art. 156 — Amended by No I of the FA of 16 Dec. 1994, in force since 1 Jan. 1997 (AS 1995 1227; BBl 1991 III 1).

:PATH
3. Distribution
|

:INFO
Art. 157
1 The costs of administration, realisation and distribution are paid in advance from the pledge proceeds.
2 The net proceeds shall be paid to the pledgees up to the amount of their claims, including interest up to the time of the last realisation and the costs of enforcement.
3 If not all the pledgees can be satisfied, the debt enforcement officer shall determine the ranking of the creditors and their shares, taking account of Article 219 paragraphs 2 and 3.
4 Articles 147, 148 and 150 apply accordingly.
|

:NOTE
Para. 1 — Amended by No I of the FA of 16 Dec. 1994, in force since 1 Jan. 1997 (AS 1995 1227; BBl 1991 III 1).
Para. 2 — Amended by No I of the FA of 16 Dec. 1994, in force since 1 Jan. 1997 (AS 1995 1227; BBl 1991 III 1).

:PATH
4. Pledge shortfall certificate
|

:INFO
Art. 158
1 If the pledge could not be realised because the bids were insufficient (Arts 126 and 127) or if the proceeds do not cover the claim, the debt enforcement office shall issue the enforcing pledgee with a pledge shortfall certificate.
2 After service of this document, the creditor may pursue debt enforcement by way of distraint or bankruptcy, depending on the person of the debtor, unless the debt is a land charge certificate (Art. 33a Final Title CC) or another real burden. If the debtor brings debt enforcement proceedings within one month, a new summons for payment is not required.
3 The pledge shortfall certificate is deemed to be an acknowledgement of debt within the meaning of Article 82.
|

:NOTE
Para. 1 — Amended by No I of the FA of 16 Dec. 1994, in force since 1 Jan. 1997 (AS 1995 1227; BBl 1991 III 1).
Para. 2 — SR 210
Para. 2 — Amended by No II 4 of the FA of 11 Dec. 2009 (Register Mortgage Certificates and Other Amendments to Property Law), in force since 1 Jan. 2012 (AS 2011 4637; BBl 2007 5283).
Para. 3 — Inserted by No I of the FA of 16 Dec. 1994, in force since 1 Jan. 1997 (AS 1995 1227; BBl 1991 III 1).

